diff --git a/packages/appium/docs/en/guides/managing-exts.md b/packages/appium/docs/en/guides/managing-exts.md
index c0dd89a52..ee3060cd8 100644
--- a/packages/appium/docs/en/guides/managing-exts.md
+++ b/packages/appium/docs/en/guides/managing-exts.md
@@ -85,3 +85,25 @@ project.
 This strategy is *only* recommended if you are already using `npm` for your project.
 Otherwise, it is recommended that you use Appium's Extension CLI and, if necessary, adjust
 `APPIUM_HOME` to change the location of stored extensions.
+
+## Embedding Appium in an `npm` Package
+
+If you publish a package that starts Appium itself, such as a tool that ships Appium together with
+the drivers it needs, the drivers can end up installed in different places depending on the package
+manager and the project that uses your package. Instead of relying on `APPIUM_HOME`, resolve each
+driver from your own package with Node.js module resolution, and pass its location in `useDrivers`:
+
+```js
+import {main} from 'appium';
+
+await main({
+  useDrivers: [import.meta.resolve('appium-xcuitest-driver')],
+});
+```
+
+A location can be an absolute path or a `file:` URL of the driver package directory, of its
+`package.json`, or of any file in the package, such as the entry point returned by
+`import.meta.resolve()` or `require.resolve()`. Appium reads the driver's metadata from the
+`package.json` of that package and loads the driver from there. These drivers are not added to
+`extensions.yaml`, and they take precedence over installed drivers with the same name or
+`automationName`. Names of installed drivers can be listed alongside them.
